{
  "name": "What to Expect During a Residential Roof Inspection",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: Ground-Level Review",
      "text": "Our inspector typically starts by walking around your home, checking the roof's perimeter from the ground. They’ll use binoculars to look for any obvious signs of damage, such as missing shingles, sagging, or issues with gutters and downspouts. This initial walk-around helps us get a sense of the roof’s overall condition and identify any immediate concerns before getting on the roof.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: Roof Surface Examination",
      "text": "If it's safe and accessible, the inspector will get on the roof to examine it up close. This is where we look for granular loss, cracked or curled shingles, damaged flashing around chimneys and vents, and any signs of wear and tear. We're also checking penetrations like skylights and pipes to ensure they're properly sealed. Safety is always our top priority, so if a roof is too steep, fragile, or unsafe, we'll use alternative methods like drones or extended cameras.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: Attic and Interior Check (If Accessible)",
      "text": "When possible and safe, we'll also check accessible attic areas. This helps us look for signs of leaks, water stains, mold growth, or ventilation issues that might not be visible from the outside. We’ll also note any visible structural conditions that could impact your roof’s integrity. This inside-out approach gives us a comprehensive view of your roof's performance.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Drainage and Gutter Assessment",
      "text": "Part of a healthy roof system is proper drainage. We'll inspect your gutters and downspouts to make sure they're clear of debris and functioning correctly. Blocked gutters can lead to water pooling, which can cause significant damage to your roofline and foundation over time. We're checking for any signs of overflow or blockages that could hinder water flow away from your home.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5: Detailed Report and Recommendations",
      "text": "After the physical inspection, you'll receive a detailed report outlining our findings. We'll clearly distinguish between observed conditions, like normal wear, and any recommended work, such as repairs or maintenance. We'll also provide photographs to illustrate any issues, so you can see exactly what we're talking about. This report is designed to help you make informed decisions about your roof’s future. Ready for an honest assessment of your roof? Contact Discounted Roofing Co. today to schedule your residential roof inspection.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "speakable": {
    "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
    "cssSelector": [
      "h1",
      ".howto-step",
      "[itemprop=\"step\"]"
    ]
  },
